# Buddy auth and secrets

Human guide: `docs/auth.md`. Session pick is `ResolveSession` in `internal/buddy/session.go`.

- Config is `context:` (`default`) + nested `orgs:` (Jamf organization → environment → product tenant) + `auths:` + optional `channels:` policies. Load expands `orgs:` into the in-memory workplace map session pick already uses. Dual-read `tenant:` / `tenants:` / `--tenant` / `tenant=` for one release; loading config rewrites those keys on disk and folds leftover flat `contexts:` into `orgs:` (invented org `default` for instance-only). Nested `orgs.*.environments.*.tenants` is Jamf’s product-tenant list, not the root dual-read. Older `credentials:` / string `tenant:` / `defaults:` / `allow_auths:` are rejected. Selection: `--context` (auto-pick a workplace name) or `--user`/`--auth` (pin). Auth `scope:` is Jamf organization|environment|tenant. Auth `channels:` limits buddy clients `cli|mcp|ide` (omit = all). Top-level `channels.<client>` sets `access` / `context` / `contexts` allowlist, optional `deny_builtins`, and optional `allow_builtins` (allow follows deny). The same deny/allow lists are valid on org/env/tenant nodes and `auths.`; layers are independent (channel allow cannot unblock a context deny). MCP always denies `jamf_*` on the channel layer unless `allow_builtins` unblocks it. `deny_builtins: ["*"]` is an allowlist at that layer. Cowboy channel override: `allow_builtins: ["*"]`.
- macOS org defaults: Jamf ACS/MCX preference domain `virtualpete.buddy.cli` (or `BUDDY_MANAGED_PLIST`) overlays onto user `config.yaml` (user wins per name). Auths in the plist are accepted if present; `buddy setup managed-plist` omits them. Prefer `buddy setup secrets` for the store. `SaveConfig` does not snapshot unmodified MDM keys.
- Token logic lives in `internal/buddy/tokenauth/`; process cache in `common.go` (`credAuthCache` keyed `auth@tenant`); disk cache in `~/.config/buddy/tokens/` (AES-GCM; Darwin wraps with Keychain account `token-wrap`, no userPresence / not the vault DEK; Linux uses compiled-in obfuscation; skipped for command auth). Debug/`--trace`/`--script-trace` go through `internal/redact` (JWTs, Bearer, `access_token`/`token`/`client_secret`); `auth token show --raw` is the explicit leak.
- Secrets: inline config wins, then `secrets.DefaultStore()` (encrypted `secrets.enc` vault; random DEK in Keychain on macOS / `dek` file on Linux). Recovery passphrase is **user-held**: wraps the DEK inside `secrets.enc` and encrypts `cred backup`/`restore`; it is **not** stored in Keychain/file (legacy `master` items are deleted once a wrap is present). Vault saves preserve an existing wrap without needing the plaintext passphrase. Cancelled presence / LA does not fall through to recovery when the DEK exists. `BUDDY_VAULT_PASSPHRASE` overrides the recovery passphrase for CI (never written; `dek:v1:…` overrides the DEK). `cred backup`/`restore` always require typed `--passphrase=` or a prompt (or the env override) — never a machine-stored default. Signed Darwin (`-tags secitem`): one `VaultStore` per process; unlock lazily on first vault-backed secret resolve (mcp/serve do not unlock at start; a valid token cache skips the vault). Prefer Keychain `userPresence` when the platform allows it; bare Developer ID CLI falls back to LocalAuthentication `evaluatePolicy` with `touchIDAuthenticationAllowableReuseDuration=0` and fail-closed if the policy is unavailable (SecAccessControl needs restricted keychain entitlements / `.app`). Escape: `BUDDY_VAULT_UNLOCK=after-first-unlock`. Linux / Windows / unsigned `./buddy` stay file/`security(1)` without presence. No PBKDF2 on ordinary vault decrypt. Hard biometrics are planned for an optional macOS Buddy Agent (Swift tray minting tokens over UDS); do not re-ship Go helper Keychain Sharing / SE as the primary path.
- Buddy Agent (`macos/BuddyAgent/` + `internal/buddy/agent`): menu-bar Swift app that **owns its own integrations** (workplace + auth + secret in Keychain service `virtualpete.buddy.agent`; config JSON in Application Support). buddy never pushes secrets to it. Newline-JSON `status`/`config`/`integrations`/`tokens`/`token`/`lock` on `~/Library/Application Support/Buddy/agent.sock` (peer audit-token codesign check: same Team ID + identifier `virtualpete.buddy`/`virtualpete.buddy.cli`). Everything but `token` is served **while locked** — identity is not secret; unlock gates minting only, and there is no `unlock` op. Protocol v0.3: `token` carries `allow_unlock` (set unless `BUDDY_AGENT_NO_PROMPT=1`) and a locked Agent raises its **own** LocalAuthentication prompt through `UnlockGate` (`AgentUnlock.swift`: coalesced, fresh `LAContext` with reuse 0, `invalidate()` timeout at `BUDDY_AGENT_UNLOCK_TIMEOUT`=60s, `BUDDY_AGENT_FAKE_PRESENCE=allow|deny` for dev soaks) while buddy waits 90s; failures are `unlock_declined`/`unlock_timeout`/`unlock_unavailable` and are **fatal** (never the vault). Success unlocks the session, which relocks on the idle timer (`BUDDY_AGENT_IDLE_LOCK`=900s, reset per mint) and drops cached bearers. Tray Unlock uses the same gate; `status` adds `presence` (`acl`|`simulated`|`unavailable`) / `idle_lock_seconds` / `unlock_timeout_seconds`.
- Agent signing (`macos/BuddyAgent/BuddyAgent.xcodeproj`, synchronized folder group on `Sources/BuddyAgent`): `make agent-app` archives + `-exportArchive method: developer-id` into `dist/BuddyAgent.app`; `scripts/build-agent-app.sh` fails without hardened runtime, secure timestamp, `embedded.provisionprofile`, and `keychain-access-groups`. `make agent-dev` is the unsigned SwiftPM loop. That profile is what makes `presence: acl` reachable: `AgentKeychain.backend` probes the **data protection** keychain at launch and stores each secret behind a `userPresence` `SecAccessControl`. A probe that fails because the keychain is not ready (`errSecInteractionNotAllowed` / `errSecNotAvailable` / `errSecAuthFailed`, or `errSecMissingEntitlement` when an access group is present) is not a memory fallback: the Agent retries on screen unlock and relaunches itself once if `kern.boottime` changed under the process or a fresh `LAContext` is still rejected (`BUDDY_AGENT_NO_RELAUNCH=1` suppresses that). Unsigned / missing-entitlement probe-fail keeps secrets **in process memory only** (no login-keychain fallback). Launch silently deletes leftover login-keychain items for service `virtualpete.buddy.agent` without reading them. `has()` never prompts (`kSecUseAuthenticationUIFail`; `errSecInteractionNotAllowed` = exists), `get()` takes the gate's authenticated `LAContext` and refuses rather than drawing its own sheet. `UnlockGate` uses `evaluateAccessControl(.useItem)` on the ACL backend and **keeps** the context as the session; `AgentState.onRelock` invalidates it on tray Lock / `agent lock` / idle relock. `BUDDY_AGENT_SPIKE_CLIENT_SECRET` is dev-only (`PeerCheck.allowUnsigned`). `make app` nests the Agent at `Contents/Library/LoginItems/BuddyAgent.app`, **verified never re-signed** (`codesign --force` would strip the profile). `make notarize` staples `Buddy.app` then the pkg; needs `BUDDY_NOTARY_PROFILE` + `BUDDY_PKG_SIGN_IDENTITY` (Developer ID **Installer**), makes the `--timestamp=none` fallback fatal, and `make release` uses it when the profile is set. Nothing may rebuild the app between those stages (phony `sync-docfs` makes a recursive `make package` rebuild and drop the ticket), so `notarize` runs the shared `BUILD_PKG` directly and `build-pkg.sh` refuses an unstapled app while `BUDDY_NOTARY_PROFILE` is set. The wait is bounded by `BUDDY_NOTARY_WAIT` (default 30m) — a timeout is "not yet", resumable with `make staple` (cdhash lookup, no resubmit, no rebuild) — and `status: Invalid` is reported separately with `notarytool log`. `pkgbuild`'s four `write: Permission denied` lines are its own noise, payload-independent. A signed Agent rejects unsigned `dist/buddy` — the Go `denied` error names `Helpers/buddy` and `BUDDY_AGENT_ALLOW_UNSIGNED=1`. No SMAppService registration, auto-start, update channel, or sandboxing yet. Token order is disk `tokens/` → Agent → vault; `agentToken` runs before `loadSecretsIfNeeded` in `tokenauth`. Agent-minted bearers are **memory-only** (never `saveDiskToken`; the mint sweeps any existing file) and `AgentOwned` skips the disk cache entirely, so `agent lock` stops the next process; `RefreshToken`/`RefreshSession` re-mint via the Agent with `force` instead of falling to the vault. Absent socket or `unsupported` falls back to the vault unless the auth is Agent-owned (`tokenauth.Config.AgentOwned`), which fails; `locked`/`denied` always **fail** (never open the vault behind a running Agent). `features.use_agent` / `BUDDY_USE_AGENT` folds `agent.Config()` into `LoadConfig` **beneath** managed prefs and the user file (`cfg.agent`, `configBaseLayer`); `features.force_agent` / `BUDDY_FORCE_AGENT` / MDM `force_agent: true` does the same but **skips the user file** (MDM still overlays; MDM true is sticky). SaveConfig never persists the Agent's entries, `cred set|delete|rename` refuse those names, `cred status` shows `SECRET=agent`, and an absent Agent is fatal when the flag is already on. Empty/missing `config.yaml` on Darwin (`Enabled()`) tries the Agent once: contactable → use in memory (do not persist `use_agent`; no workplaces → error that the Agent needs config); absent → first-run setup with a Buddy Agent hint. Other platforms keep the chooser. `BUDDY_NO_AGENT=1` is the kill switch, `BUDDY_AGENT_SOCK` overrides the path, `buddy agent status|config|integrations|tokens|lock` (alias `buddy auth agent`) is the smoke test, `buddy agent enable|disable` writes `features.use_agent` to config.yaml, `buddy agent force [--off]` writes `features.force_agent`, `buddy agent prune [--dry-run] [--secrets]` drops user-file entries the Agent also serves (parents wait for their children; `cred status` flags the shadowing). `buddy setup export --json` is the identity-only GUI import format; the GUI also imports a `buddy cred backup` file (identity + secrets) by decrypting that envelope itself in `AgentBackup.swift` — payload keys pinned by `TestCredBackupPayloadKeysForAgentImport`. No entitlements on the Go CLI; unsigned SwiftPM Agent means memory-only secrets (no `userPresence` ACL).
- Resolve via `CredentialConfig.ResolveAuthWithSecrets` (composed tenant+auth view) before building an `Authenticator`.
- CLI: `buddy auth token show|token status|token drop|token refresh|test|authorization`, `buddy token direct`, `buddy cred status|add`, `buddy cred set context|auth|channel|secret|passphrase …` (`--channels=`; `tenant` is a dual-read alias), `buddy cred delete context|auth|secret …` (parent with children is blocked; cascade + store secrets), `buddy cred rename context|auth`, `buddy cred restore-config`, `buddy cred clean secrets|tokens`, `buddy cred erase secrets`, `buddy cred backup|restore <file>`, `buddy cred use [--cli|--mcp|--ide]`, `buddy cred store`, `buddy cred migrate`. First-run: `buddy setup` (chooser), `buddy setup export [path]` (secrets-free YAML), `buddy setup import <file>` (YAML/plist), `buddy setup secrets`, `buddy setup managed-plist`. `cred add --paste` / `--from=` match import/paste.
- Token refresh: reuse until expiry (minus ~15s skew). Keep-alive is **basic auth only** on 401 or `buddy auth token refresh` when a cached token is still usable (otherwise obtain). OAuth/platform `auth token refresh` replaces via client credentials (also on expiry or 401). Authenticated HTTP retries once on 401 after refresh. Disk token files are purged when expired (`cred clean tokens` or on load miss); `auth token drop` invalidates still-valid cache files. CLI, mcp, and serve load vault secrets lazily only when minting/refreshing a token (valid disk cache skips Touch ID). `cred status` does not open the vault unless `--unlock` or `--test`.
- Live checks: `buddy auth test` always ForceRenews (instance and platform). Probe 403 after a minted token is `live=token-ok`, not a bad secret. Organization workplaces probe `GET /licensing/v1/licenses` (403 → token-ok); environment/tenant workplaces probe buildings. Last results + Jamf authorization details (`GET /api/v1/auth`) are cached under `~/.config/buddy/caps/` and used by session pick (fail open). `cred get secret` is presence only.
- buddyscript/MCP session kwargs are `context?` / `tenant?` / `surface?` / `auth?` (`cred` removed; `tenant?` dual-read). Soft-switch on auth pin alone is a fallback when the implicit context is not linked. A readonly pin falls back to a full-access auth on the same context for mutates; channel `access: readonly` does not. Classic follows a platform pin by default (`/proclassic/…` with `/JSSResource` stripped). Set `features.classic_instance_only: true` or `BUDDY_CLASSIC_INSTANCE_ONLY=true` to force Classic onto instance. That toggle is Classic `/JSSResource` only. ACS `/api/config-profiles/` and undocumented Pro `GET /api/v1/policies` are always instance (Jamf Pro UI / unpublished JSON; not on the gateway). When the selected context is an environment that cannot serve the op (no platform auth, that Classic lock, ACS, undocumented policy list, schema), session pick uses the first Jamf Pro product-tenant child with an instance URL and instance auth. `auth test` and Platform product APIs do not walk down.
